    <h3 class="landmark heading" id="work">Chapter Text</h3>
    <p>Alphas were meant to be at the peak of the hierarchy. They were meant to be strong, fierce, unyielding leaders who claimed the head of every table.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Of course, time passed, and modern society has slowly moved away from that archaic brand of chest-thumping ideology. But not Max. It was probably one of the few lessons his father had managed to permanently stick into his brain.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And no matter what way he looked at it, he knew one thing for sure.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George Russell was no Alpha to him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A fake retching sound drifted in from his left. Daniel, the bastard, apparently decided that bothering him was a better use of his time than hanging around the McLarens.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Mate, you really need to keep your pheromones in check."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't even bother looking in his direction, preferring to narrow his eyes at George, who was prancing around the paddock like a gazelle.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He really didn't understand how the guy ended up presenting as an Alpha.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Aside from his unnaturally large blue eyes and tall stature, nothing about George screamed "leader" or "dominant". Well, except maybe his sharp mouth or quick hands. Those were lethal enough, he supposes. He would know. Max has been on the receiving end of it ever sinceâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Careful, Max. Poor Georgie's gonna turn into ash if you keep glaring holes into him."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shrugged off the arm draped over his shoulder with a scoff. "Poor Georgie got out of a car and smacked someone. I think he can handle a little glare."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There's only one reason for George to be loitering around the Red Bull motorhome, and both of them knew it. Alex Albon.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The same Alex Albon who was his teammate for one season. A season and a half, if you want to be technical. The one George had defended in the media, saying that Red Bull was trying to "make him look like an idiot and he's absolutely not."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Honestly, if Albon wasn't a Beta, Max would have bet good money there was something going on between the two of them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George walked past him without even a single glance, and right on cue, there it was again. That irritating scent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>To keep any drama or scandal from spilling over within the Formula series, especially after that Schumacher mind-games thing with Rosberg, the FIA made scent blockers mandatory for all drivers â€” whether they were an Alpha, Beta, or Omega.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Normally, George would have carried a trace of crisp rosemary and bergamot with him. But thanks to that stupid plastic patch on his nape, his natural scent was flattened into something artificial.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Down the hallway, Albon and George were already hugging it out like they didn't see each other literally ten minutes ago. Max rolled his eyes so hard he was sure he strained his optic nerve.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>If it wasn't Albon, then it was Lando. If it wasn't Lando, then it was this hyperactive honey badger stuck to his side right now. It was like the brunette couldn't survive a day without clinging on to someone.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>From where he was standing, Max could see Albon give George a friendly pat. Only for that hand to slide down George's back, settling just above his asâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't even need to hear Daniel gag beside him to know that his scent had just gone sour again.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Seriously, Max? What's the big deal with George?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Get off me." Max finally tore his gaze away from George as he walked out of the motorhome. "And like hell if I know. Just looking at him pisses me off."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He didn't have a better explanation to give, so Daniel was gonna have to be satisfied with his answer, whether he liked it or not.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>People parted like the Red Sea as his heavy stomps echoed through the paddock. He ripped out a fresh scent-blocking patch from his pocket before slapping it callously on his neck.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Seeing George with Albon had already ruined his day. He wasn't about to give the FIA an excuse to fine him for it too.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Secondary Sex<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody"></span></p><p><strong>Secondary sex</strong> is the biological classification established by the <span class="articlelink">secondary-sex determination system</span>. It is distinct from <span class="articlelink">basal sex</span> (male, female, intersex), which refers to an individualâ€™s primary sex characteristics and associated biological development. Secondary sex refers to a coordinated set of anatomical and physiological characteristics determined by a separate genetic system.</p><p>The three recognized secondary sexes are <span class="articlelink">Alpha</span>, <span class="articlelink">Beta</span>, and <span class="articlelink">Omega</span>. </p><hr class="wikihr"></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The boy in white stepped out of his kart, taking off the brightly colored helmet from his head. Max watched as the boy slightly tipped his head back, blond strands falling perfectly into side-swept bangs.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It should have made the boy look like a coconut.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It didn't. Staring at him, starstruck through his visor, little Max's first thought was surprisingly simple: <em>'Woah, pretty.'</em> Then the second thought hit him like a punch to the gut: <em>'This fucker just beat me.'</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't move to antagonize him, but he didn't exactly soften up either. He couldn't. Not when he still felt the crushing weight of his father's expectations, and definitely not after a loss like that. So he firmly kept his helmet on, using it like an armor as he braced himself for the strike that he knew was coming.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The boy in white got to him before anyone else could. Up close, he stood an inch or two taller than Max. It was a subtle difference in height, but it was enough to make him bristle like a cat.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max blinked through the dark tint of his visor. The fog in his head cleared up just enough for him to recognize who this was. <em>He's not blond at all</em>. <em>The sun had tricked him</em>. This was <em>that</em> boyâ€”the pretty boy who was constantly around Albon. Though he would argue that "pretty" was almost an understatement.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The boy was breathing heavily, sweat dripping down his forehead, and his face flushed red from the adrenaline. But what caught his eye was that smile. It was both blinding and steady, completely untouched by the sheer brutality of the race they'd just been in.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was disorienting. In a world full of egos and posturing, the boy's stance was open and devoid of any arrogance that Max came to expect from his competitors.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A fireproof glove appeared in front of him, offering an eager handshake.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Hi. I'm George. You were amazing!" The pretty boy gasped out. "Seriouslyâ€”That was the most fun I've ever had in a race."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't get any time to respond before the boy took off, running his mouth a hundred miles an hour.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>In fact, George was talking so fast that his hands started joining in, animatedly gesturing in the air to illustrate a race that they both participated in. In that time, he pulled back the hand he offered before Max could even shake it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So Max stood there trapped, trying to understand the sudden barrage of words, but he found that George had a weird way of speaking English. <em>Kind of like Albon</em>. Certain vowels were drawn out with a specific enunciation, while others were slurred together and trailed off with an endearing lisp.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Mid-gesture, George froze, eyes widening as he came to a realization. "Oh, dear! I didn't even ask for your name! Sorry. That was quite rude of me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Max." The words came out flat and completely muffled by the helmet.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George tilted his head while leaning into his space. "Huh?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"My name is Max."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Secondary sex-determination system<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody"></span></p><p>The <strong>Secondary sex-determination system </strong>is a biological <span class="articlelink">sex-determination system</span> present in many <span class="articlelink">mammals</span> (including <span class="articlelink">humans</span>), some reptiles (<span class="articlelink">Lacertilia</span>), and some snakes.</p><p>In this system, the <span class="articlelink">secondary sex</span> of an individual is determined by a pair of <strong>inherited alleles </strong>designated <strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, and <strong>O</strong>. The system classifies individuals into three principal phenotypes: <span class="articlelink">Alpha</span>, <span class="articlelink">Beta</span>, and <span class="articlelink">Omega</span>. Each phenotype is further classified into genetically defined subtypes.</p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="leftsection"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/bwhBnZf.png"> Mechanism</p></div><div class="leftbody"><p>All <span class="articlelink">animals</span> have a <span class="articlelink">genome</span> made of <span class="articlelink">DNA</span>, which forms <span class="articlelink">chromosomes</span> during cell division. In humans, most mammals, and some other species, the system is associated with two chromosomes, called the <span class="articlelink">Ackles chromosome</span>and <span class="articlelink">Padalecki chromosome</span>. Each of these chromosomes carries a copy of the gene that codes for the secondary sex. In these species, the combination of alleles inherited through these chromosomes determines the secondary sex of the offspring. Offspring usually inherit one allele from each biological parent, as with other diploid genetic systems.</p><p>The system consists of three alleles:</p><ul>
<li><strong>A (alpha-associated allele): </strong>Promotes Alpha differentiation</li>
<li><strong>B (beta-associated allele): </strong>Promotes Beta differentiation</li>
<li><strong>O (omega-associated allele): </strong>Promotes Omega differentiation when present in two copies.</li>
</ul><p>The resulting allele combination determines the individualâ€™s secondary-sex genotype and classification.</p><table class="genotype-table">
