</table><p>The <strong>BA genotype </strong>is classified as Beta because the B-associated allele takes precedence over the A-associated allele. In <strong>AO </strong>individuals, the A-associated allele is expressed despite the presence of one O allele. Similarly, the B-associated phenotype is expressed in <strong>BO </strong>individuals. The O-associated phenotype is expressed only in individuals with the <strong>OO genotype</strong>.</p><p><strong>Relationship with basal sex</strong></p><p>Secondary sex is genetically distinct from <span class="articlelink">basal sex</span>. An individualâ€™s chromosomal classification does not determine their secondary sex genotype. Similarly, the secondary sex does not establish an individualâ€™s chromosomal complement, gonadal anatomy, reproductive capacity, or fertility.</p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max isn't stupid.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Looking back on it, he knew exactly what kind of game George was playing. Coming up to him after a loss, putting himself near Max, striking up a conversation right when Jos began marching up to him. It was George's clumsy way of "protecting" him from a public beatdown.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>As an adult, Max could almost appreciate the gesture for what it was. But back then, to a little him whose entire worth was tied to his dominance, George's protection felt like a slap to the face. It felt like George took a look at him and decided that he was too soft and fragile to fight his own battles.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Every time George stepped in, he was showing the entire paddock that Max couldn't even handle his own life.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They'd argued about it once. The resentment had built up in his heart, and it all boiled over after a bad race.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max wrenched his hand back from George with enough force to throw them both off balance. He stood rigidly as he glared with sheer rage. "I don't need your help," Max snarled, taking a step back to create more distance between them. "Stop acting like that. I'm not your fucking charity case."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George didn't even flinch. He simply rolled his eyes, making Max feel like an idiot for lashing out in the first place.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George puffed his chest out, thumping a gloved fist over it with pride. "I don't think you're pitiful at all. My parents just taught me that Alphas are meant to protect other people. You can't defend yourself against your dad yet, so I wanted to protect you even through a small thing like this."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Alpha.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Hearing that word drop so casually from a kid his ageâ€” who hadn't even grown into his shoulders â€”made Max's stomach turn.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Of course the Russells had prepared for their boy to become an Alpha. Why wouldn't they? Given their family pedigree and the older children falling neatly in line, becoming an Alpha wasn't a question for George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was just Max who refused to accept it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Deep down, he didn't understand his own resistance. George becoming an Alpha was supposed to be a good thing. Omegas were weak, helpless, little things incapable of real contention. If George turned out to be an Omega, they never would have stood as equals, let alone rivals. The competition would have been over before it even started.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George caught his confused look and immediately became defensive. For all his charm, George was sensitive about the way people looked at himâ€”with his wide, bright eyes and fine features, he looked more like a fairytale prince than a ruthless racing driver in the making.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What's with that look?" George crossed his arms tight over his small chest. "What? You think I won't become an Alpha too?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I never said that, dumbass."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Then stop looking at me like that." George huffed. His cheeks adorably puffed out, making him look as intimidating as a duck.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max couldn't help it. The dramatic display only deepened his skepticism, but it did make his lips twitch.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>In a fit of childish frustration, George stuck his tongue out at him. "Also, <em>you're</em> the one who's a dumbass! Just watchâ€” I'm going to present first, and then I'm going to tell you <em>'I told you so'</em> until your ears bleed."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That was how the argument ended, with George turning around and pulling him along.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This time was no different. George was dragging him again through the bustling paddock, babbling about getting their pictures taken in front of their karts before the sun set.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was deaf to the noise around them. His eyes were glued to the hand that was clutching his. They passed by so many people, even bumping into someone's shoulder without a word of apology. But he was too engrossed in his own private game. Every few steps, he would squeeze George's fingers tight in his palm, then let go to watch the pale skin bloom into a blotchy shade of red.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They finally stopped in front of Mrs. Russell, who was holding a camera in her hands.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Her face lit up with a welcoming smile. "There you two are." But her next words died in her throat the moment her eyes dropped down to their joined hands. Alison stared at her son's trapped hand, then up at the intense focus in Max's eyes as he flexed his grip.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Little Max didn't miss the way her smile faltered, hardening into a frozen mask.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Mum! Could you take a picture of us?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George's mother paused as she adjusted herself. "George, dear, let go of Max's hand." She sounded gentle in a way Max had never heard an adult be before. "You're holding him too tightly. Look, his knuckles are turning white."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh." George glanced down to look at their laced fingers, sheepishly smiling at Max before letting go. Max couldn't stop his brows from furrowing as the warmth left him. "Sorry, Max."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max tucked his hands behind his back so nobody could see them twitching. "â€¦It's fine."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Mrs. Russell gave them both a small nudge. "Go on, pose in front of your karts now. Max's parents must be wondering where he went. We don't want them worrying, do we?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George seemed to have forgotten his mother's first order. The second he moved, he took Max by the wrist, dragging him into position on the tarmac, and throwing an arm over his shoulder to pull him close.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They did all the ridiculous poses that they could think of: squatting down in front of their karts, posing with double peace signs, and clumsily doing the 'fusion pose' from an anime that George saw.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But the one Max held closest was a picture taken mid-air. In it, their hands were loosely intertwined, swinging back and forth between them until they both jumped, smiling only at each other.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A week later, an envelope came to Max's house with Mrs. Russell's handwriting on it. Inside were all of the pictures that they took. It was the first of many polaroids that he ended up saving over the years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid1"><div class="polaroid1_front"></div><div class="polaroid1_back"><div class="polaroid1_message"><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span class="colortext1">Another amazing week racing with Max. I wonâ€™t lose next time.</span></p><p><span class="colortext2">We look stupid. Not happening any time soon, Princess. Pick a different dream.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="polaroid2"><div class="polaroid2_front"></div><div class="polaroid2_back"><div class="polaroid2_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext1">Double win!!! Congrats to both of us!!! (we got ourselves chips)</span></p><p><span class="colortext2"><strike>You looked</strike> Good job (itâ€™s called fries british boy)</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The kid who bumped into him was gone before Max could even yell.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George had to go to the 'loo' and left his green and red helmet with him. So for the past few minutes, Max was standing outside the toilets, tapping his shoe impatiently as he waits for George to finish. His own white helmet sat on one hip and George's on the other.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That was probably why it was so easy for someone to knock one out of his hands. It hit the floor with a loud bang, startling everyone in the near vicinity.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>By the time Max knelt down to pick it up, the asshole had already run. <em><span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">Klere.</span><span class="translate-alt">Cholera.</span></span></em> There was quite a large, ugly dent at the very top of the helmet. No matter how hard Max tried to push it, it just wouldn't budge.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He glanced to his left and right, trying to see if George had come out yet. <em>What am I going to do?</em> He couldn't return it looking like this. George had trusted him with it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Somewhere behind the wall, the sound of a flush hit his ears, and Max did the first thing that came to mind.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He ran.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Running with two helmets made him exhausted, but it would all be worth it if he could fix this thing before George found him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Alex!"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Alex, who was drinking, almost spat out his water at Max's shout. "What?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shoved the green helmet into his face. "It's broken. It's <em>broken</em>, Alex! You have to help me fix it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Alex frowned while taking a step back to see it properly. "Okay. Well, first, calm down."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I <em>am </em>calm."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Right." Alex winced at seeing the dent before smoothing his face into a wobbly smile. "It's not that bad. I mean, it's not <em>that</em> broken."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"<span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">Vernield, deuk,</span><span class="translate-alt">Destroyed, dented,</span></span> it doesn't matter." Max waved around the helmet. "Help me fix it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Er. How exactly am I supposed toâ€”"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Alex. Have you seenâ€”"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max heard George pause mid-sentence, and he had to stop himself from physically shrinking. He immediately hid the evidence behind his back, though it was futile. "Erm."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George pressed his lips into a thin line, holding out a hand before saying, "Max. Give it to me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max hesitated.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Max."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>At George's second call, Max waddled forward and handed it over with his head down. He mumbled his way through explaining what happened, pouting at the floor, unable to stop his lip from wobbling. If he were George, he would definitely think these were all just excuses.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He was so sure that George would leave without a word. Instead, there was a soft sigh, followed by a pat to his hair. "It's fine. Don't worry too much about it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max's head snapped up immediately. "Really?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George chuckled at him and ruffled his hair. "Yeah. It's fine. It's just a helmet."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon couldn't help but interject. "You <em>slapped </em>me for accidentally getting ketchup on it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George had this pleasant smile on his face, while Albon beside him was twisting away in agony as he was pinched hard at the ribs. "Don't listen to him, Max. It's really fine."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was unsure, but he still ended up nodding. If George says it's fine, then it's fine.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George left soon after that, and he watched him go with the dented helmet tucked under his arm. The knot between his shoulders finally came loose.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"He's going to cry about that," Albon muttered, rubbing his side.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"He said it's fine."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon looked at him for a long moment. He opened his mouth, closed it, and tried again. "Yeah. He did say that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid3"><div class="polaroid3_front"></div><div class="polaroid3_back"><div class="polaroid3_message"><p><span class="colortext2">I almost had it. I was so close. I just needed one more try.</span></p><p><span class="colortext1">Dear diary, some idiot tried to apologize by getting me a plushie. He didnâ€™t win one. I had to drag him away.</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid4"><div class="polaroid4_front"></div><div class="polaroid4_back"><div class="polaroid4_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext1">Apparently he went back to try again and won. I wanted to name it Pebbles, but he vetoed it. Its name is Pingo now.</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max had never hated being small more than he ever did in this moment.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George won again. He'd driven as beautifully as always. That's why, this time, Max worked up the courage to approach him first. Walk towards him, clap him on the back, and say <em>congratulations</em>. That's it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It's been 5 minutes now.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max is still standing at a distance with his helmet hanging in his hands. He watched as George was slowly but surely swallowed by the crowd. Some of them were people Max knew and some he didn't. Max tried calling out to him once. Then, again. But no matter how hard he tried, it just didn't work. George was always too occupied, the room was too loud, and Max was too short to be seen over anyone's shoulder.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was <em>so</em> frustrating. It left him feeling raw and hollowâ€” an ache curling beside his ribs where anger bled into hurt.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Eventually, the commotion died down. Their eyes met between the gaps once, and George's whole face immediately lit up. He flashed him a wide grin while waving around the trophy over his head.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George beckoned with his free hand. <em>Come here</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Finally</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He didn't waste a single second.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max dropped his helmet and came rushing down towards him. Thankfully, he stopped himself just in time before he crashed into Georgeâ€” who was panicking the entire time, wondering if he was gonna have to catch a raging bull.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They looked at each other again, Max breathing heavily, George wide-eyed and clutching the trophy in front of him like a cross. <em>Fuck</em>. One after another, they burst out into giggles.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You look stupid."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You look <em>stupider</em>."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What were you doing all the way over there? You should have just run up to me like that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max pursed with lips. Maybe he should have. But seeing <em>that</em>, seeing George get passed around from one person to the next, made him realize something. There are so <em>many</em> people in George's life. They <em>admired</em> him, <em>adored</em> him, and <em>wanted to be near</em> him. Max wasn't anyone special.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He caught himself wondering where he fit in the grand scheme of George's life. Was he just another familiar face in the crowd? A friend? A competitor to beat?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max is scared of what George's answer would be.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Yeah." Max let out a breath. "Next time, I will."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p><img src="https://www.google.com/images/branding/googlelogo/2x/googlelogo_color_272x92dp.png" class="google-logo"></p><table class="google-search">
