<iframe class="mobilevideo" width="560" height="315" src="cid:frame-E3ADA2347C5D90C9A110DD4598E8F42E@mhtml.blink" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe>
</p></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A sharp whistle cut through the muffled hum of the corridor. Several heads turned around in mild curiosity towards the sound, but only one of them reacted the way Max wanted.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George didn't hesitate. He'd learned to pick out that sound after hearing it over and over again across different tracks and countries. It was Max's signâ€” a habit made after realizing that shouting over the engines was a lost cause. It was his wordless way of demanding George's undivided attention.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Once he heard it, George turned his entire body to fully face the Dutch. Like clockwork, he would give him that soft, private smile as he instinctively bent down to bring his ear level with Max's lips.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That single inch height difference had always annoyed Max, but in this moment, it didn't make him feel so small.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George's posture was a work of art. Even bowed at an angle, there was something mesmerizing in the way he held himself. Max's eyes would map the long line carved into his back, all the way from his tailbone to his delicate throat.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max only muttered three words to him: <em>"Good race. Congrats."</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That's all.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Even though he bothered George for such a trivial matter, George didn't seem to mind in the slightest. He was still drunk on the thrill of his own success, soaking in the praise. He nodded, laughed, and let himself be waved off by Max.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max watched him go. He watched as George was immediately surrounded by their friendsâ€” that midget Lando jumping up and down, Charles talking with that loud ass voice, and Albon slinging an arm around George's neck.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't mind it this time. That surprised him a little.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Let them have him for now. George could stand in the middle of a hundred people, and all it would take was one whistle for him to turn around.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George would come back to him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He always will.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max scrambled down the steps to dig his camera out of his mother's bag. It was a sleek silver Kodak digicam that he'd found in a dusty corner of the attic. It had taken a fresh set of batteries and a lot of begging before his mother finally let him keep it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>From across the paddock, he aimed the lens straight at George, who was laughing with a group of people. He was mid-laugh, head tipped back, and hair flowing freely in the air when Max pressed the button.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The flash went off.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Unfortunately, he had forgotten to turn it off. A few people in that direction turned with barely hidden smirks. So now, Max was frozen, fumbling at the buttons of his camera, hoping to look like someone who was definitely not taking pictures of George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George noticed. <em>Of course</em>. He was already grinning from where he stood, and Max felt heat crawl up his neck.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What 'chu doing?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shoved the camera behind his back. "Nothing."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"That," George pointed at the silver casing peeking past Max's hip, "doesn't seem like nothing at all."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Why did you bother asking me then?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I need to know if I at least looked good in it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I wasn't taking your picture."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Right. And I'm RuPaul." George snorted, rolling his eyes at him before closing the distance to swipe the camera from his hands. Thankfully, Max saw through it and quickly ducked out of reach. "Come on! Let me borrow it for a bit."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Why?" Max tried not to sound exasperated, and mostly failed. "What are you even going to do with it?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I'm going to serve it over a net," George said flatly. "<em>Duh</em>. What do you think I'm going to do with a camera, you numpty?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And that was how it all began.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>From then on, Max found himself running around with George, taking pictures of anything and everythingâ€” the karts, the stray cat, the food, the track at the golden hour. It became their routine, repeating itself race after race.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George took to it faster than Max thought he would. Sometimes he'd bark orders like a director, <em>"Get my good side," </em>and pose like he was going to be on a magazine cover. One hand tucked under his chin, looking far away for a "candid" shot, or smiling with his eyes squeezed shut. Max never once said no, and he'd rather eat dirt than admit how much he loved doing it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Sometimes, George would borrow (steal) the camera to take pictures of Max instead. The pictures are either of Max up on the podium or pulling some stupid face George found hilarious. Every so often, he took one just because he thought Max looked good.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max noticed, with some offense, that this doesn't happen a lot.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They would take pictures of their other friends too. Like when Albon won first place, Max and George stood in the front row to document the moment. Though he had to admitâ€” he wasn't really paying attention to Albon. He was too busy holding back his laughter at seeing George beside him making a serious face.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid5"><div class="polaroid5_front"></div><div class="polaroid5_back"></div></div><div class="polaroid6"><div class="polaroid6_front"></div><div class="polaroid6_back"><div class="polaroid6_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Never cut your hair.</span></p><p><span class="colortext1">I just cut it. Heâ€™s literally crying.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="polaroid7"><div class="polaroid7_front"></div><div class="polaroid7_back"><div class="polaroid7_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Horrible picture. Iâ€™m never doing this again.</span></p><p><span class="colortext1">Aww. But you looked so cute...</span></p><p><span class="colortext2">Oh.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="polaroid8"><div class="polaroid8_front"></div><div class="polaroid8_back"><div class="polaroid8_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Stupid Charles. Stupid Albon. Iâ€™m going to learn how to play too.</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Presentation<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody"> </span></p><p><strong>Presentation </strong>is the process of physical changes through which a <span class="articlelink">child</span>â€™s genetically determined <span class="articlelink">secondary sex</span> becomes apparent. Although secondary-sex genotype is established at birth, the associated characteristics remain largely incomplete until their presentation. It is initiated by <span class="articlelink">hormonal</span> changes that occur during the height of <span class="articlelink">puberty</span>. In response to these changes, the expression of <span class="articlelink">secondary sex-determination genes</span> increases, stimulating the growth and transformation of the brain, <span class="articlelink">bones</span>, <span class="articlelink">muscle</span>, <span class="articlelink">skin</span>, and <span class="articlelink">secondary-sex organs</span>. The resulting changes allow the individualâ€™s secondary sex to be identified through physiological examination and <span class="articlelink">scent recognition tests</span>.</p><p>On average, <span class="articlelink">females</span> begin presenting at ages 10Â½ to 17; <span class="articlelink">males</span> begin presenting at ages 12 to 17. The progression of presentation differs according to the individualâ€™s genotype. A major landmark of presentation for <span class="articlelink">Alphas</span> is the development of their <span class="articlelink">alpha-associated canines</span>, which occurs on average around 6 months after presenting. For <span class="articlelink">Omegas</span>, the development of oestral secretory activity (commonly referred to as <span class="articlelink">slick</span>) occurs on average around 3 months after presenting. <span class="articlelink">Betas</span> are characterized by the limited development of their specialized secondary-sex organs.</p></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George presented first.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The moment he did, all the adults who'd spent years betting against the "princely boy" swallowed their own words. They still had their doubts, but at least they had the sense to stop saying it to his face.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But where George began to blossom, Max was left to rot.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Inside Max's house, Jos became a ticking time bomb. He grew stiffer and harsher, with his face locked permanently in a scowl whenever George's name was spoken.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Because even after a month of waiting, Max still hadn't presented.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Suddenly, those vile, hungry eyes that followed George found a new target, and it was unfortunately Max. Marshals, sponsors, and rivals were all watching him like a rare specimen, waiting to see if he would crumble.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max kept his chin raised. He <em>knew</em> he was an Alpha. He could feel it deep within his bones. But as the days dragged on with no sign from his own body, that unshakable confidence curdled into anxiety. And anxiety, in Max's blood, always burned straight into anger.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His face sent such a clear warning that it made everyone turn away. At moments like this, there truly is no doubt that he was his father's son. That thought only added to his displeasure.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He found himself sulking at the end of some dingy hallway, tucking his knees against his chest while glaring at nothing in particular.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Someone stepped in front of him and blocked out the light. George looked amused, his hands casually resting on his hips. "What are you doing?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Leave me alone."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Ah." George sighed playfully. "Teenage rebellion, is it?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"We're the same age, asshole."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Clearly, I'm more mature than you are."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That struck a nerve. "<em>Yeah," </em>Max said, voice dripping with acid. "You sure fucking are. I'm just so fucking immature and dysfunctional compared to <em>Princess George</em>, aren't I? Are you happy now?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The Brit winced, holding up both hands. "Christ, who pissed in your drink?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Go away."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George didn't. He even went out of his way to squeeze right next to him, folding himself to imitate how Max sat. George nudged his shoulder. Max nudged him back harder.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>They kept it going at it for a whole minute until George actually lost his balance and tumbled over dramatically.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>God, this is stupid.</em> He looked down at George, who was groaning against the floor. A reluctant smile tugged at his lips. <em>This is so childish.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George jabbed a finger right between his eyes. "Stop that. You look horrible."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Like I give a fuck."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Is <em>fuck</em> the only curse word you know?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Was that a challenge?</em> Knowing George, the answer is obviously yes.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"<span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">Kut! Schjit! Kanker! Schitjgloeiendekankergodverdommeâ€”</span><span class="translate-alt">Cunt! Shit! Cancer! Shit-glowing cancer goddamn itâ€”</span></span>"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I get it. <em>I get it.</em> Can you shut up now?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So he did. He didn't feel like talking anyway, and George was the stupid one for approaching him when he was in a shit mood. <em>See, I know other curse words.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You know, presentation usually occurs between ages twelve and seventeen for males," George started talking like he was reading from a textbook. "There are other factors that can affect it such as environmental, physical, emotionalâ€”"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I fucking know that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George scooted himself in front of Max and rested his chin on his knees. He tilted his head, eyes melting into something sickeningly sweet. "Then quit moping. You'll present too. No matter what happens, you'll always be Max to me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max kept his mouth shut, helplessly frustrated as he stared back at George. <em>That's not it. That's not what I want you to say.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His eyes drifted downward, tracing the collar of George's jacket, fixated on where his scent gland was safely tucked away. It had become a sick habit that he couldn't break ever since George presented.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He hated that it was different now, and he hated himself more for checking whether it still was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Always be Max. Who exactly is "Max" to you?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Because to him, "George" seems to be more than a friend or rival. With George, a boring race could turn into something thrilling. With George, even the dumbest jokes could be worth laughing at. With George, Max is left feeling restless and hollow whenever his gaze wanders.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There was no one else who could trigger that frantic, fluttering pulse underneath his skin.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"George" seems to be his idolâ€” his light, his ruin, his muse.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And maybe in a few weeks, months, or years, his presentation would decide what that meant.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He already knew what his father expected. Anything else would have been a weakness. If Max presented as an Alpha, he and George would be the same type. Their names would always be chained together as friends, rivals, equals, and nothing more.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>An Alpha status would result in his loss, ending any quiet dream of pursuing George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But a Betaâ€” or worse â€”then maybeâ€¦ just maybeâ€¦</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Maybe that would be his chanceâ€¦</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A cold shiver ran down his spine as the thought slipped through the cracks. His whole life, the word <em>Omega</em> was spoken with the same disgust reserved for <em>failure</em>. What he was now bargaining for was something that he'd been raised to despise. The wish made him feel filthy, but a small, pathetic part of him wished for it anyway.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His questions were finally answered on the night before a race in his junior career.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was twelve when he woke up suffocating in bed. The room felt like an oven, with all the air turning thick and rotting with a dozen overlapping scents coming in from the street outside. A violent wave of nausea hit his throat, and his loud retching shattered the silence of their house.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He heard his mother running down the hallway. She managed to yank the door open, but she didn't make it to his side.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Instead, she stood there, horrified by the heavy leather and raw petrol slamming into her senses. It was her son's wild and unrefined scent. She clamped a hand over her mouth and nose, wide-eyed and helpless as Max began clawing at his neck and chest from the agonizing heat inside his veins.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His father was even worse. He was clearly capable of lifting Max off the floor or cooling his feverish skin, but all he did was watch. He didn't move an inch even as Max looked up at him through a blur of tears.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Jos' lips curled into a slow, satisfied smile.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>His son wasn't broken. He was an Alpha</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Years later, even with his memories muddled by the haze, that was the one thing that burned itself permanently into his brain.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Jos was hell-bent on getting him in that track. So, he dragged Max into the car anyway, indifferent to what was clearly a miserable presentation.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His mother and sister flanked him in the back seat. Driven by instinct, his mother moved to wrap him in her arms, wanting to pull his head against her chest so her scent could take the edge off his fever.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max barely managed to twist away before he retched right over his knees. It broke his heart to push his mother awayâ€” he could see that hollow, exhausted look in her eyes â€”but the closed space made it hard for him to breathe.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>To his right, Victoria <em>(smart girl) </em>understood him without a word. She chose to simply pat his back and let him calm down on his own.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Suddenly, his father's fist slammed against the empty seat next to him, instantly killing every sound in the car.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Everything after that was a fog, except for that one brief stop that they took. Much to his father's chagrin, they pulled over into the parking lot of a roadside plaza. His mother stepped out of the car and returned a minute later with a pink-and-white <em>diphenhydramine capsule </em>and a cold bottle of water. She quickly moved, popping the pill into his mouth and tipping the water down his throat to help him swallow it. At the same time, his sister reached over to press a scent blocker over his nape.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The medicine took hold of him <em>fast</em>. His eyelids felt heavy, yet every time his mind started to float away, Jos' erratic driving snapped him right out of it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>When he finally stepped out of that car, the scents nearly knocked him off his feet.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It wasn't as bad as it had been when he presented earlier, but it was barely an improvement. He was tired, feverish, and agitated. His mind wasn't as sharp as he would like it to be, but it'll have to do. After all, there are no easy roads on the way to the top. He <em>had</em> to drive through it. He <em>had</em> to win.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was strapped into his kart, pulling his helmet down tight over his head. Before he knew it, the race had already begun.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The kart lunged forward as he clutched the steering wheel. This time, the layout was simple. In the past, he had dismissed the track as monotonous and boring, but today, its simplicity was his only saving grace. The straight stretched on for so long, giving him time to reset before slamming the brakes just in time for the hairpin.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Every vibration of the engine rattled through his teeth, spreading into his aching joints. It was, without a doubt, one of the most grueling races he had ever endured.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His hands wouldn't stop shaking. The pill was still burning through his system, making him drive more aggressively than usual.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He crossed the line a few seconds ahead of the pack. Maybe he could get away with saying he has a winner's blood running through his veins, and not liquid fire.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The illusion broke the moment he hauled himself out of the kart and took his helmet off. He was met with people around him bursting into gasps and whispers.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This race nearly killed him. His skin was burning under the suit, and the air around him was growing heavy.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He registered the sound of approaching steps, but Max could smell who it was before he even saw a shadow. He could never mistake it for anyone else. Suddenly, the roar of the crowd faded into static, and all he could feel was George's presence.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George was panting hard, standing over him with his face twisted in concern. He cupped Max's cheeks in his warm palms, speaking of something that Max simply couldn't process.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Catching the blank look in his eyes, George cursed under his breath and quickly rummaged through his pockets, pulling out a clean handkerchief.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George held it to his nose.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max slapped George's hand away immediately. He had just finished dragging himself and that kart across the finish line. He refused to be coddled like a five-year-old who scraped his knee.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There wasn't any more movement on George's side. He just stood there with his hand still half-raised and a look on his face that Max had never seen before. It was <em>fear.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Why are you looking at me like that?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Something warm ran over his lips. He touched it without thinking, and his glove showed up with a dark, wet spot.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ah</em>. Max finally understood what was wrong. He watched the red bloom across the pale blue handkerchief that George pressed back to his face. <em>My nose is bleeding</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't stop him this time. George's fingers were gentle at the back of his head, tilting it forward. And when the world tilted under his feet, George's free hand caught him by the elbow and held him there.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The walk towards the clinic passed by in half-remembered flashesâ€” the corridors blurred together, the cold air from the AC hit his skin, and those stupidly bright fluorescent lights blinded him. Every step landed too loud and every smell was too much: antiseptic, floor polish, and someone's burnt coffee.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He couldn't hold on to any of it. It all came too sharply and too fast, and it slipped away from him before he could make sense of it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Except for one thing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was faint at first, so Max thought his feverish brain had imagined it. Then the scent flared again, much closer this time, pulling his face toward it before he even had the sense to resist. Next thing he knew, his cheek was pressed against George's chest. It was softer than he thought it would be. It was strange. <em>Squishy. </em>One would think George had a bit more hard muscle with all that karting.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>So that's what he smells like</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max's fingers closed around the hem of George's sleeve without noticing. His eyes grew heavy as he finally sank back into the white mattress. For the first time since he presented, nothing hurt.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He closed his eyes as he let himself bask in the warmth of George's presence.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>How nice.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="disciplinary"><div class="disciplinary_cover"><div class="disciplinary_cover_text_top"><p>Northgate Medical Center<br>
Department of Otolaryngology<br>
Tel: 0991-XXX-XXXX<br>
28 June 2009</p></div><div class="disciplinary_cover_text"><p align="center"><br>
Verstappen, Max Emilian</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_top"><div class="disciplinary_top_text"><p>Patient Name: Verstappen, Max Emilian<br>
Age: 12<br>
Basal Sex: Male<br>
Secondary Sex: Alpha, AO<br>
Date of Examination: June 2009<br>
Attending Physician: A.J. Anderson<br>
</p><p>Diagnosis: <b>Acute-onset Presentation, Pheromone Hyperosmia</b></p><p><br>
</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_mid"><div class="disciplinary_mid_text"><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Patient presented within the last 72 hours, with onset of fever, myalgia, and marked sensory sensitivity to odor, light, and sound. Onset was abrupt and was not followed by supervised rest. Patient competed physically during the acute phase. Post-exertion epistaxis was noted. Patient was seen by a medic on site before referral.</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_bottom"><div class="disciplinary_bottom_text"><p>Recommendations:<br>
</p><ul>
<li>Rest and observation for a minimum of 7 days. No competitive or strenuous activity.</li>
<li>Follow-up in 4 weeks to reassess olfactory thresholds.</li>
<li>Counselling on presentation for patient and guardians. Patientâ€™s guardians are advised to allow him a familiar scent source during recovery, as this has proven effective in managing acute symptoms.</li>
</ul><p align="right">Signed: A.J. Anderson, M.D.</p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max had yet again buried his face deeper into George's chest. His hands locked tight around his waist as if holding onto an anchor in a storm. He took a deep breath, filling his lungs with that familiar herbal scent that never failed to soothe his nerves.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Here, in George's arms, the rest of the world faded into nothing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>After a beat, a pair of arms circled around Max. George drew him in and began patting and stroking his hair in slow, rhythmic motions. He closed his eyes as George gently combed his fingers through the strands, his nails lightly scratching at his scalp to melt away all his tension.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon stood there awkwardly at the side, darting his eyes back and forth between them like he couldn't quite process what he was looking at. He had been talking with George earlier before Max came in and interrupted them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Through the haze of his overstimulated brain, Max saw George shoot Albon an apologetic smile but didn't push Max away. Albon seemed to realize that he wasn't going to win this implicit tug-of-war for George. And so, he chose to just quietly resume where they'd left off rather than making a scene.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Good</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max <em>needed </em>this. George was the only reprieve he had in this godforsaken paddock. Without him, this stupid illness would have turned every single race into pure torture.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Everyone else in here smelled fucking repulsive. Their sickeningly raw pheromones clung to the back of his throat like thick dust, driving him completely insane.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The specialist back at the clinic tried to explain the phenomenon to themâ€” talking about some <em>receptor inhibition</em> and the <em>chemical blocking mechanism </em>of George's scent. He honestly didn't give a fuck about whatever that fancy medical jargon was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>All he heard was that George was <em>his cure</em>. And he wasn't about to let him go.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Charles had presented much later than the two of them and was now officially an Alpha.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Yes. </em>That's right. Charles Leclerc is an alpha. Very surprising. Though nobody was more surprised than Charles himself, who had spent weeks complaining about it to anyone who would listen.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I didn't ask for this," he said for the fourth time that day. Then, he draped himself all over George's side, throwing the back of his hand over his forehead like a Victorian maiden. "Ugh. George. Darling. How are we ever going to get married now?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Crrk</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The violent crunch of an aluminum can snapped everyone out of their senses.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max's Sprite was now a crumpled little thing in his fist, spraying clear carbonated liquid down his wrist. He was looking at it with such disinterest that one would think the can did that on its own.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"So," Lando, sensible as he is, immediately tried shifting the attention away from the mangled Sprite can. "Charles, any changes yet? Can you feel your canines growing?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Charles is, unfortunately, staring shitless at Max's hand and cannot answer him properly. He was still for a few seconds before slowly removing himself off George and scooting away.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George reached over and slapped Max on the back of his head.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Ow."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I bought you that drink with my own money, and you're just going to trash it?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"It tastes like shit."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Why on earth did you pick that then?!"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Because I wanted to try it!" Max took George's handkerchief and dabbed at his wrist. "Plus, you were standing next to me the whole time, going 'just get it, just get it.' You were pressuring me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Off to the side, Charles managed to crawl his way next to Albon. He clung to his arm with a dramatic shudder. "A-Alex," he whimpered, throwing in a fake quiver. "Those two are insane."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon patted his head sympathetically, closing his eyes in solemn agreement. "There, there. Sometimes, you just have to ignore those crazies."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I don't know, man," Lando interjected, taking a long sip from his Fanta. "You were kinda asking for it when you proposed in front of Max."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I was joking."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Sure you were</em>. Max sneered inwardly while keeping his focus locked on his ongoing argument with George. Charles must take him for an unobservant idiot if he thought Max was buying that rehearsed line.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>With Albon, Max didn't care. Anyone with half a brain could tell that they really do see each other as friends, maybe even brothers. But Charles? Charles was a different story. That fucker wanted the same thing Max did. He could <em>feel </em>it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That golden boy was playing up being a dramatic little shit, using every excuse to throw himself at George. And that stupid French cheek-to-cheek custom? Charles didn't lay a lip on him or Lando or Albon, but he suddenly turns into a traditionalist whenever George is within arm's reach. And he was fucking <em>Monegasque</em>!</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Behind the bench, Charles suddenly retched, cutting their argument short.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando shot up to his feet in disgust. "Charles? What the hell?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Charles waved him off and took the handkerchief that Albon handed him, wiping his mouth clean. "Sorryâ€” Sorry. I'm still not used to the pheromones thing. Something smelled really off just now."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George pursed his lips and subtly gave Max a look. Max exhaled through his nose.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Sorry."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The apology was flat and about as sincere as the government. Charles accepted it anyway with a tired wave. He really didn't want to bother with whatever obsessive claim Max was trying to stake over George. <em>Can't even have a happy crush anymore because of woke.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The arcade was loud and bright, packed with kids, students, and a hundred different screens. It was a rare miracle that he and George had been allowed to hang out outside of the track, and Lando, who could never let him be happy, had intercepted them at the gate and dragged the whole group here.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Fine.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max walked straight past the claw machines towards the one game that he was more familiar with. <em>Initial D Arcade Stage 8 Infinity</em>. He, Max Verstappen, who spent most of his days going in circles on a track, was finally out of the paddock and decided to play a game about street racing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He wasn't ashamed of that. He'd practically been born into this sport. Of course he loved it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He slid into the seat, gripped the wheel, and hogged it. Max wasn't really hearing any complaints. In fact, a small crowd had gathered behind his seat just to watch him play.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Five races in and the screen flashed another win. It was fun at first, but there was nothing that could beat the feeling of winning a real race. So he got up and left. A few people behind him groaned about it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Now, this was where Max's hyperosmia came in handy. It was the one perk of presenting that he'd kept to himself. In a room that was this loud and crowded, one scent cut clean through it all: that crisp, green fragrance with a fresh bite of herbs and sun-ripened peel.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was quite a convenient thing. Max didn't even have to look for him anymore. All he had to do was follow his scent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Thanks to that, he saw that George had also gathered a crowd, but for the shooting game.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George had the fake gun raised to his right eye, one arm straight and the other one braced, holding it perfectly like he was shooting an action film. The screen lit up with confetti for every bullseye. After each one, George would slightly lower it and flash a grin over his shoulder. It was quite a charming smile that said, <em>"Aren't I something?" </em>And without fail, a wave of squeals would come out from the people watching.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max stopped at the edge of the crowd with his hands in his pockets.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>These people have no decorum</em>. The two boys at the front were filming on their phones. A girl beside them was marching in place with a hand on her cheeks. The scene looked like something you were more likely to see at a concert than an arcade.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Seriously. He's a minor. Leave him alone, you freaks.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shifted his weight, his shoe tapping repeatedly against the tile as the crowd grew louder, wilder, and more suffocating by the second. Driven by a sudden impulse, he pursed his lips and blew a distinct, piercing whistle.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Even over the noise, the sound seemed to have reached where George was. He whipped his head around, scanning the room before his eyes locked onto Max. In an instant, the charming, performative grin on George's face faded into something softer. He could practically hear what George was saying with that look.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Oh. There you are.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A few disappointed 'awws' rippled through the crowd, but they still parted for him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Five seconds.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>As soon as George reached him, Max pulled him in by his hand, propping his chin on George's shoulder. He let out a long sigh, grumbling into George's ear about how unbearably noisy it was. His eyes swept across the crowd, taking in every jealous gaze, before he closed his eyes and sank deeper into the curve of that neck.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Five seconds. That's how long it took for his prince to come to him. <em>Not bad</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid9"><div class="polaroid9_front"></div><div class="polaroid9_back"><div class="polaroid9_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">There was a new shooting game where the seat vibrated when you got shot. Whoever created that shit better watch themselves.</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid10"><div class="polaroid10_front"></div><div class="polaroid10_back"><div class="polaroid10_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Spending money on a photobooth when we could have just used my cam smh</span></p><p><span class="colortext1">Donâ€™t be cheap</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What is it that you like about George?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Mind you, Max was peacefully eating a blueberry cheesecake that he bought for the sole purpose of finding happiness after a shitty race. He was already halfway through it when Lando decided to pull this shit out of nowhere.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He swallowed first, because his cheesecake deserved the respect. "I like all my friends."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando snorted. "Come on. Just tell me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I don't like him like that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Stop lying out of your mouth," Lando started hitting his back hard, making him choke on a graham crumb. Max is starting to realize that Lando was genuinely frustrated and really needed his answer for some goddamn reason.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Still. Even if it was important, Max was a little hesitant to answer. "You won't tell him?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh myâ€”" Lando stopped himself from cursing. "Yes, muppet. I quite enjoy watching you humiliate yourself. Now, can you answer?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max gave him the finger, then actually thought about it. <em>What is it that he liked about George?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There were just so many reasons why. He doesn't even know where he'd start.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>'He's hot' </em>was the easiest one to admit. But that wasn't what made Max keep thinking about him. It was the fights. The stupid ones that started over nothing, like a lap time or the last bite of something, and the way George never let him easily win them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George would dig his heels in, snap back with something sharp, and look so pleased with himself that Max wanted to shove him and kiss him at the same time. Everyone else gave way eventually. George made him <em>work </em>for it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And then there were the quiet parts that nobody else saw. The moments where, ten minutes after a fight, they would sit side by side and share a wireless earphone. The lazy afternoons where Max would lay down his head on George's lap and listen to him read a book. Or when they would simply hand each other a cold water bottle after a race without needing to say a word.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando didn't need to hear all that.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"He's hot."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando looked at him with enough disgust to curdle his cheesecake. He pinched the bridge of his nose. "You're so sleazy."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What? It's not the <em>only </em>reason I like him. And what's so wrong about liking someone for their looks?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Why did you have to start with that?" Lando held up a hand. "Let me change the question. When did you fall in love with him?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I'm not in love with him." Lando glared at him, and Max had to backtrack a bit. "But to answer your question, probably when we were kids. Like when we were running around the paddock taking pictures of everyone."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando's eyes lit up.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Or maybe it was when I saw him surrounded by so many people," Max went on, because apparently he was doing this now. "He's charming. Everyone wanted a piece of him, and I wanted to be someone he picked out of the crowd."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ew. Cheesy. </em>Max took a bite of his cheesecake to wash his mouth off the cringe.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando was leaning in, nodding along to every word. "Okay. <em>Okay.</em> So it had nothing to do with his type, right?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"The hell?" <em>Where was this conversation going? </em>"What does his type have to do with it?"
</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Yeah." Lando nodded absentmindedly, mostly to himself. "You're right. Types have nothing to do with it. You can just love anyone for who they are."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Okayâ€¦ </em>There really was more to this. Max thought Lando was just being nosy, but this guy was clearly working through something and was using Max as his test subject.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Well, yeahâ€¦" Max said slowly, "It's not like I have an Alpha fetish or something. It's just George."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando's face crumpled at his words. "There's a fetish for that?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max narrowed his eyes. He remembered the Australian Beta Lando had been hanging around with all year. He took another bite and shrugged. "There's a fetish for anything. Alpha, Betaâ€¦"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando was gone before he could finish his sentence.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max watched him run in distress and went back to his cheesecake. <em>So his hunch had been right.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid11"><div class="polaroid11_front"></div><div class="polaroid11_back"><div class="polaroid11_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Lando left as soon as â€œOscarâ€ came. No loyalty that one</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was sixteen when England finally legalized same-sex marriage. George made sure he heard all about it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max swallowed his water, tilting his head as he watched him. "Isn't that a good thing?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Well, of course it is." George leaned back against the wall, letting his gaze float towards the ceiling. "But it feels like such a <em>'damn here'</em> law that they threw just so they could pretend they care about equality. And the whole thing barely makes any sense. Same sex marriage is legal, but only if it's an Alpha and Omega pairing. Do you know how ridiculous that is?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Yeah, actually.</em> <em>It is a bit ridiculous</em>. Where Max came from, same-sex marriage and secondary-sex laws were legalized in one go. He doesn't understand the point of such rigid, arbitrary rules. <em>Seems a bit backwards to me.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George dropped his head onto Max's shoulders with a loud sigh. "Do you want to know what argument they used?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max tilted his head and rested it on George's hair. "I could guess."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"'God is the Alpha and the Omega. The beginning and the end. Out of His divine grace they were made; therefore the Alpha shall seek out the Omega, and in their union shall His righteousness be fulfilled.'"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ah. Revelation 1:8 and Genesis 1:26-27.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Didn't they use the same argument against the gays?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George sat up straight, eyes lighting up in agreement. "Exactly! The amount of stupidity gathered in our parliament is mind-boggingly ludicrous."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Debates over attraction and dynamics are nothing new. For decades, society has argued whether we should orient based on our basal sex or secondary sex.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Given the label "secondary sex", one could already hazard a guess. If you mapped out the world's acceptable pairings in a hierarchy, it would probably look a little something like:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>â€ƒ 1. Alpha Male + Omega Female</p><p>â€ƒ 2. Alpha Male + Alpha Female</p><p>â€ƒ 3. Beta Male + Beta Female</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Huge fucking gap</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>â€ƒ 4. Alpha Female + Omega Female</p><p>â€ƒ 5. Alpha Male + Omega Male</p><p>â€ƒ 6. Alpha Female + Omega Male</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Another huge gap</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>â€ƒ 7. Any Beta-Alpha or Beta-Omega pairs</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And at the bottom of the barrelâ€¦</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>â€ƒ 8. Same-sex Alpha-Alpha and any Omega-Omega pairings</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And God, that was just the basics. Society didn't even have a category for transgender dynamics.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Why are you so bothered by this anyway?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George huffed. "Perhaps I'm simply a man of profound empathy."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>What a ridiculous thing to say.</em> "Oh, of course. My apologies, <em>Princess</em>. Have you considered moving to somewhere more progressive? <em>BelgiÃ«</em>, perhaps?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George snorted and reached out to flick his forehead. "Hard pass. Though, I appreciate how dedicated you are to planning my emigration. Not happening, though."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>A shame</em>. Max didn't even know he was expecting an answer until he heard it. He let himself imagine what it would have been like: George in his country, in his city, in his home.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He would have given everything for George to just be there. Instead, all he had was this endless waiting and hoping.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Look at how low he had stooped</em>. Max Verstappen, motorsport's rising star, reduced to playing chemist in his bedroom, mixing synthetic oils just to get a poor imitation of George's scent. Reduced to a hollow stalker rotting in bed, devouring every article, every interview, and every photo like a starving man digging through scraps.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Look at me. Look at what you do to me.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Even then, Max gave nothing away. He stayed silent and let George burn himself out on a political rant. He leaned his head back against the wall, tracking every movement of George's with unblinking eyes.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This fragile closeness they had came with an expiration date. Sooner or later, their career paths would diverge, and one of themâ€” maybe him, maybe George â€”would walk away without looking back. But right now, Max wouldn't let that happen.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He rubbed the tips of George's brown hair between his fingers. <em>What do I have to do to keep you here with me?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Marking<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody">
</span></p><p><strong>Marking</strong> is a form of temporary <span class="articlelink">social</span> and <span class="articlelink">biological signaling</span> in which one individual leaves a recognizable bite-shaped mark over another individualâ€™s <span class="articlelink">nuchal gland</span>. The mark remains visible for a limited time and fades as the affected skin heals and returns to its normal appearance.</p><p>Marking is practiced among all <span class="articlelink">secondary sexes</span>, though its social meaning varies with the relationship between the individuals involved. The bite is typically placed at the base of the nape, directly over the nuchal gland, where the <span class="articlelink">pheromones</span> are strongest and the skin is thinnest. Unlike <span class="articlelink">bonding</span>, a mark does not alter the biology of either participant and carries no lasting physiological effect.</p></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This was probably one of the worst things that Max would ever have to go through. George had come to him for help. And Max was <em>so </em>willing, so eager to say yes before he even knew what he was supposed to help with.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Iâ€”I want to confess to her."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't have to ask who <em>her</em> was. It was a girl from his neighborhood or something. He'd tried listening to him once, really, when George was talking about her with Albon. But halfway through, he found himself standing up and muttering some random excuse to leave.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon watched him go with a look that was equal parts suspicious and pitying. Fuck him for that, by the way.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That's why his first question was, "Why are you asking me?" Then, because it sounded too much like he cared: "Actually, what are you even asking me for? Do I look like a relationship guru to you?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George pouted his lips, and it was so ridiculous and so cute and so fucking effective that Max had to look away. "Well, I can't ask Alex or Lando. They'll make fun of me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What makes you think I won't?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George lowered himself until they were eye to eye, deliberately fluttering those eyelashes while tilting his head. "Will you?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>You little shit. You know what you're doing.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was irked. Max was also already holding a piece of paper.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That's how he found himself helping George practice his confession. He stood opposite George, reading the notes that he made. "Why do I have a script?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Because you're going to say ridiculous stuff."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"So? What if your crush says ridiculous stuff?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George huffed at him. "She's not like that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Oh, please</em>. All George did was defend her lightly, and Max still wanted to disagree on principle. Whoever this girl was, she can't be any good. George can be oblivious, after all, considering he's still this close to Max despite his shenanigans.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max rolled his eyes at George's stiff, awkward stance. "Whatever. Let's just get this over with."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The Brit breathed in, breathed out, and opened his eyes to look straight at him. If Max were this girl, he would have said yes in a heartbeat before George even finished the first word. Max shoved that thought where he didn't have to deal with it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Maxine." <em>What</em>. "Can we talk for a minute? I have something I want to tell you."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I'm busy."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Max!" George's indignant shout rang in his ear, making him block the noise out with his fingers.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Jesus. I was joking. Loosen up a bit."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Okay. Again. And I'm being serious this time." George glared at him until he was sure that Max would behave. George took another breath. "Maxine, can I talk to you for a sec? I have something I want to tell you."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max debated saying something stupid again. In the end, he still read his line, but with a flat, disinterested tone. "Sure, George. What is it that you want to say?" <em>What in the grammar school response is this?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But George actually looked nervous, like he meant every single word. "You and I have known each other since we were kids." I <em>bet I know you better than she does</em>. "And even though I'm always away for races, you somehow made me feel like I'll always have a home to come back to."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>If you let me, that person could just be me.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Max." <em>What</em>. "Iâ€”" <em>What</em>. "I really like you a lot, Max."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>What</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I know we're both Alphas, butâ€¦"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Everything turned to static, and Max didn't hear whatever else George was talking about.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Did he just say 'Max'? What kind of evil fuckery is this? Was his crush's nickname 'Max'?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His heart was beating so hard, thumping so loudly against his chest that he was scared George could hear it. His throat suddenly felt dry as he kept swallowing around nothing. He couldn't read the next line on the paper.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He opened his mouth anyway.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I like you too, George."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The silence stretched on for so long. They stared at each other, unreadable emotions swirling in both of their eyes as they processed what just happened. Then, George cleared his throat.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You were supposed to say, 'Sorry. I don't see you that way.'"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Well, that script's stupid." Max's voice came out higher than he meant it to be. "Why the hell did you get rejected in your fictional scenario?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"So I can prepare. Obviously."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shoved the damn paper back into his hands, trying to get away from here as fast as possible. Anywhere is fine. Anywhere but here. "Fuck. Go practice with Charles or something."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Homotypicality<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody"></span></p><p><strong>Homotypicality </strong>is <span class="articlelink">romantic attraction</span>, <span class="articlelink">sexual attraction</span>, <span class="articlelink">courtship</span>, or <span class="articlelink">sexual behavior</span> between people of the same <span class="articlelink">secondary sex</span>. It also denotes <span class="articlelink">identity</span> based on attraction, related behavior, and <span class="articlelink">community</span> affiliation.</p></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="wikipedia"><p><img src="https://i.imgur.com/BmP2UTB.png" width="25%"></p><p><span class="articletitle">Insipid<br>
</span></p><hr class="wikihr"><div class="row"><p><span class="articlebody">
</span></p><p><em>Insip redirects here. For the adjective, see Insipid (adjective). </em></p><p><strong>Insipid</strong>, often shortened to <em><strong>insip</strong></em>, is a <span class="articlelink">slur</span> in the <span class="articlelink">English language</span> used to refer to <span class="articlelink">homotypical</span> individuals and couples. The slur is historically used to claim that homotypical pairings possessed an inferior capacity for <span class="articlelink">pheromonal bonding</span> and lacked the biological compatibility to support a legitimate partnership and procreation. Today, it is widely regarded as <span class="articlelink">derogatory</span> and is considered <span class="articlelink">hate speech</span> in several jurisdictions.</p></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was still sixteen when George got into his first relationship.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He stood on edge, listening to the nauseatingly intimate sounds of George making out with some Omega he'd fished out from some back alley inâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>The bitch just moaned</em>. Max felt his blood pressure spike. A fucking stranger from who-knows-where was getting off on itâ€” reveling in what was supposed to be Max's. Every gasp from the other side felt like a physical blow.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max bit his lip hard, clenching his fists as his eyes reddened at the edges. This was so unfair. Why do other people get to have George? Why do they get the privilege of being with him for being a different sex?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He stormed out before he did something drastic.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando came across him first. It took one look at his bleeding palm and suffocating aura before Lando decided to change his approach, holding out his hands in a placating manner. "Woah. You good, Max?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Who is that bitch?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando blinked, utterly baffled by a question that came out of nowhere. "<em>Excusez-moi?</em>"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Who's the one making out with George right now?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando immediately brightened up at hearing such piping hot tea. "Holyâ€” George is making out with someone? Where?" He almost started to look around the hallway before his brain fully processed those words. His excitement vanished into thin air the moment he realized he was standing in front of a ticking time bomb. "I meanâ€” <em>wow</em>. Shit. I have no idea, man."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Fuck you're useless." Max ignored Lando's indignant shouts behind him, already marching down the corridor to look for the only other person in this paddock who would actually know something.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon was talking to another rando when Max saw him near the entrance. He honestly doesn't give a fuck about irrelevants like that. Max stepped right into their space, reaching out to pull Albon by the shoulder and force him to make eye contact.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Do you know who George is dating?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Whaâ€”" Albon stumbled a bit in his attempts to pull back.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Just answer the fucking question." Max cut him off, tightening his grip on that shoulder. "Who's that fucking Omega George is making out with?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon was about to open his mouth. But before he could respond, that fuckass rando had the nerve to chuckle into his hand. "No way," the guy sneered, his voice dripping with condescension, "Russell is with an Omega?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max turned to him, glaring. "None of your goddamn business."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The rando didn't care and just kept talking shit. He shrugged off Max's glare and the look Albon gave him. "I'm just surprised. I thought that <em>insipid</em> princess was rolling<em> </em>around with you."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Albon turned all the way around, shaking off Max's hold on him. He caught the guy by the collar as he spoke. "What the fuck did you just say?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Are you deaf? I said I thought George was rollâ€”"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max never heard the rest of that sentence. He didn't hear the end of it, and later on, he wouldn't remember hearing the start.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The first sense to come back was his hearing. Something wet landed heavily on the floor. Then, it was quiet again.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>When the rest of his senses came back, he was kneeling on the floor with the guy's shirt bunched up in his right hand. He realized soon enough that his left arm was rising. It was slick and dripping. It seemed like it had been for a while, but he didn't know for how long.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The face under him didn't look like a face anymore. One eye was swollen shut into a purple bulge. His nose was crooked, and blood ran from it and from his mouth in thick lines, pooling on the ground. His head lolled with every little movement Max made. Actually, the guy wasn't moving at all.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max felt his knuckles screaming at him. And there was also this needle prickling at his aching thighs. But whatever. The asshole's clearly doing much worse than he is.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Heh.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Hands suddenly clamped around his shoulder before he could throw another punch.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Max. <em>Max</em>." Albon's voice was cracking as he used all his strength to pull him back. "He's out. He's out, you goddamn psycho! Let go!"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lando was holding his other arm, scrunching his face from the terrible sourness of his scent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max finally looked up.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Everyone stopped. <em>Everyone</em>. From mechanics, sponsors, parents, and fellow drivers. Most of them had their palms clamped over their mouths, either from the stench or what they were looking at. Some were brave enough to hold their phones up, intent on recording the whole thing despite how gruesome it was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Nobody was moving towards them, like they were afraid Max might snap and bite again. Of course, that also meant no one was moving to help the guy on the ground either.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max looked down at his hand. The awful part about this was that, even years later, he'd run this scene over and over again and think that he wouldn't change a thing. If anything, he would probably punch the bastard preemptively before he could say a thing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And then he saw George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He was standing in front of the crowd, panting as if he ran from the whole length of the paddock. He looked horrified. He was looking at Max like he was seeing him for the first time. Like he was seeing something wear Max's face. That guy George was making out with was there too, clinging to his arm and trembling in fear.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Not like that. Don't look at me like that</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That was the last thing he saw before he found himself sedated by that fucking midazolam injection.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Through all of it, Max forgot why he'd come looking for George in the first place.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="disciplinary"><div class="disciplinary_cover"><div class="disciplinary_cover_text_top"><p>FÃ©dÃ©ration Internationale de l'Automobile<br>
<b>OFFICE OF THE DISCIPLINARY COMMITTEE</b><br>
Tel: 0967-XXX-XXXX<br>
16 August 2014
</p></div><div class="disciplinary_cover_text"><p align="center"><br>
Mr. Johannes Franciscus Verstappen<br>
Ms. Sophie-Marie Kumpen<br>
Van Amersfoort Racing
</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_top"><div class="disciplinary_top_text"><p>Reference No.: FIA-DC/2014/0092<br>
Subject: <b><u>FORMAL REPRIMAND AND FIRST WARNING FOR IMPROPER CONDUCT</u></b></p><p>Dear Mr. Verstappen, Ms. Kumpen, and Guardians,<br>
This letter is a formal reprimand regarding the conduct of driver Max Verstappen toward fellow driver Nikita Mazepin.</p><p><b>I. Incident Summary</b><br>
In August 2014, a physical altercation occurred between the above-named drivers at NÃ¼rburgring, Rhineland-Palatinate, following the 2014 FIA Formula 3 European Championship. Both drivers sustained injuries.</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_mid"><div class="disciplinary_mid_text"><ul>
        <li><b>Nikita Mazepin</b>: a concussion, a dislocated nose, and severe swelling around both eyes. Mr. Mazepin has not regained consciousness and has been unable to give a statement.</li>
        <li><b>Max Verstappen</b>: minor injuries, including a fractured wrist.</li>
      </ul><p><b>II. Proceedings</b><br>
Mr. Verstappen maintained during the meeting that Mr. Mazepin instigated the incident by directing a hateful slur at a fellow driver, which provoked his response. Written statements from several drivers who were nearby at the time supported this account.</p></div></div><div class="disciplinary_bottom"><div class="disciplinary_bottom_text"><p><b>III. Findings</b><br>
The Committee considered all available statements. While it acknowledges the provocation described, it finds that the force used by Mr. Verstappen was disproportionate to the circumstances.</p><p><b>IV. Decision</b><br>
Mr. Verstappen is hereby issued a <b>First Warning</b> under Article XX. This warning will be recorded on his competition license file. Any further breach of conduct may result in additional sanctions, including suspension or revocation of license.</p></div></div></div><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article1.png" alt="Max Verstappen to Become the Youngest Formula One Driver"></p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article2.png" alt="Max Verstappen Joins Toro Rosso Amid Disciplinary Investigation"></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max lay down in his bed, surrounded by everything he had of George. The photos, the trinkets, the stickers, and the cut-outs of news reports.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He picked up one of the polaroids. It was a picture of George on the balcony, his eyes closed, the last of the sunset pouring gold all over his face. Max took it without asking, and George never knew. He looked as beautiful as always.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His chest ached, and he pressed the heel of his palm against it. His knuckles were still bruised, and it hurt more than it should have.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>That's it then.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It's been twenty-three days, four hours, sixteen minutes, and fifty-five seconds since that incident. There have been no letters, no calls, and not even one of those stupid one-word messages George would send when he was tired.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>I don't get it.</em> He turned the photo over in his fingers. "You said I'll always be Max to you," he muttered bitterly. "You told me that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So what was this? George knew Max, didn't he? George had known him since they were seven. He knew Max had never been like that, that he'd never gone that far. George should have looked at what happened and seen the reason he had for it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max didn't do it for himself. He'd done it all for George. That <em>nobody </em>threw <em>that</em> word in the air about George, and Max simply made him stop.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>If only he could explain. If he could find a way to talk to him alone for five minutes, in person, then maybe George would see. He had to. He always did.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Unless George didn't want to.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Maybe George wasn't confused, and maybe he wasn't angry. Maybe George heard people talking again and decided that it was easier to just stay away. Maybe he'd looked at all the blood on the floor and thought, <em>I don't want to be near that.</em> Maybe he just didn't want to be close with Max anymore.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His thumb pressed on the edge of the polaroid.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Is that it?" Max pointlessly whispered into the empty room, to that golden boy in his hands that couldn't give him an answer. "Is that really it?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That's ridiculous. George should have learned by now to filter out whatever outsiders try to say about him. It's all bullshit anyway. And why is he only shutting out Max? So it was fine to ignore Max, but when it's fucking Charlesâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Someone snatched the photo out of his hands.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria held it up with two fingers, nose pinched, like she was holding a dead rat. She looked disgusted. "You stink. The whole house stinks."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max tried to snatch it back from her. "Give that shit back, <span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">ezel</span><span class="translate-alt">donkey</span></span>."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh, please." She wasn't even looking at him anymore. <em>"<span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">Je zou seks hebben met een ezel als George er een was.</span><span class="translate-alt">You would have had sex with a donkey if George were one.</span></span>"</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She started clearing his bed, dropping everything she saw into a pile inside the cardboard box. Over her shoulder, Victoria started lecturing him about controlling his scent, about "being goddamn pathetic", and about how it was "no wonder George is ghosting you."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max threw a pillow at her head for that one.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What do you know?" Max petulantly turned to face the wall. "He'll talk to me again. I just have to explain it all to him."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He felt the mattress dip, and then he got slammed heavily by Victoria, who jumped him. Max let out a genuine groan of anguish. "Get off me, you damn pig."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Do you even hear yourself?" Victoria had him pinned with her elbow. Her voice dropped from mocking to something much worse. "You're delusional, brother. And stupid. You have more important things to worry about."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What could possibly be more important than this?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Hello?" She honestly sounded incredulous. "Maybe your first race with Toro Rosso? Do you know what kind of hoops Dad went through to get them to look past your disciplinary action?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max scoffed. "They'll all shut up once I get in that car."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria pulled a face at him. Her mouth was hanging open, her eyes drooping to perfectly portray both her shock and disgust. "You're <em>so</em> arrogant."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I have reasons to be."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"God. You're so insufferable, I forgot what point I was going to make." She was tapping her chin while Max stared at her, irritated and confused. "Then get out? Why are you even bothering me?" He grabbed the nearest pillow and hit her with it, then a sock, then a rolled-up Nightwing Volume 4 #134.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria sat on his legs and took every hit without blinking. She was looking up at the ceiling when she suddenly snapped her fingers. "Got it!" Her segue finally arrived. "You know how I had that dolphin stuffed toy I was obsessed with when I was little?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Was?</em> In Max's humble opinion, she was still young. Then he started realizing where this conversation was going. "I'm not obsessed with George."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I didn't say that"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You were about to!"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You didn't even let me finish!" Victoria groaned and flopped her hands into the air. "Whatever. Anyway, I took that dolphin with me everywhere. I slept with it, I ate with it, and it had to stay with me all the time, or else I would lose it." She smiled, and her voice was a lot gentler now. "Years later, I found that dolphin again in one of our storage boxes."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max gets it. He does. He hated the sentiment too. The idea that the world simply moved on and expected you to move with it. On principle alone, he wanted to go against it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria climbed off him, where she'd been sitting like a sack of bricks, and went still when she noticed that he'd stopped fighting her. "I'm telling you this right now," she said. "Just focus on your races. George's going to be another one of those faceless childhood friends you barely remember, and some things are meant to be that way. People come and go, brother."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What do you even know? You're twelve."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She threw the headset that was sitting on his nightstand. "Asshole."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What prompted you to say this?" He rubbed the spot where she hit him. "I almost threw up when I realized you were trying to give me a serious talk."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria threw her hands in the air and rolled her eyes. "Oh my god, why did I even bother? It's because you look so damn miserable all the time. Plus, you fucking stink."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Language!"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The door slammed shut behind her.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max lay there in his now "cleaned" room. He had never felt so alone. Everything with George's name or face on it had gone into the box she'd marched out with. And he let her take it just like that.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There was only one polaroid left on his bed. The one he'd managed to slip under his pillow before she came in. The very first one. Two kids holding each other's hands as they jumped into the air.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He pressed it flat against his chest.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He didn't want to move on.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article3.png" alt="George Russell wins McLaren Autosport BRDC Award for 2014"></p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article4.png" alt="Max Verstappen Makes History in Sepang: P7 Qualifying Paves Way to Youngest-Ever Points Finish"></p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article5.png" alt="Mercedes Junior George Russell takes GP3 Title"></p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article6.png" alt="18-Year-Old Max Verstappen replaces Daniil Kvyat at Red Bull mid-2016"></p><p><img class="article1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/08549487fdf319ed44845b46210e28a72f4393c4/Article7.png" alt="Kimi Raikkonen Issues Warning Over Verstappenâ€™s Aggressive Style"></p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria was wrong. Max didn't move on. Or maybe it was more accurate to say he couldn't.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It took four full years after his own debut before George finally reached Formula 1.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He caught the scent first before he saw him. Bergamot and rosemary, cutting clean through the paddock's usual stench of rubber and fuel.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max had fantasized about this moment more times than he could count on a dozen hands. He'd practice it in front of the mirror: how he would react, how he would smile, and what he would say first. There used to be a stack of paper inside his drawers, letters piled high, every one of them full of things he'd wanted to tell him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He turned around, finally able to see George again in person. It was a washed-out, sad little shade of blue that did nothing for his skin. Something much darker would have suited him. A deep navy, say, like the one Max was wearing. He'd give the white racing suit a few points, though.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was hyping himself up, working so hard not to look too eager as George walked in his direction. George wasn't looking yet, but surely he would. He would.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Then George walked past him without an acknowledgement. Nothing but that fucking PR smile he was sure he'd perfected after signing that slave contract with fucking Torger.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>It's fine. It's fine. It's fine. It's fine. It's fine. It's fineâ€”</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Maybe George was just shy. He couldn't have forgotten Max that easily. Max made sure of that. He'd won countless races, broken records, and done things that were impressive for his age, all so his name would reach the news. So it would reach George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Or maybe he was just busy. He had been talking to some girl earlier, hadn't he? Probably one of Williams' staff.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>When Max got back to his room, the first thing he did was hurl a vase against the floor.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George had changed. Unlike his more charming and extroverted personality during their younger years, he seemed to have become more withdrawn. He picked who he approached and only talked to them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Given who he's talking toâ€” Alex, Lando, Charles â€”Max figured it was only a matter of time before he got his turn. But no. Multiple races passed by and there was still nothing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This waiting was starting to make him anxious. George couldn't have possibly forgotten about him. He remembered Charles, for God's sake, and Max had definitely been closer to him than that Ferrari driver ever was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He was being singled out.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That was the only explanation he could come up with. How else could George have gone that long without saying a word to him, when they were in the same paddock every weekend?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Okay, maybe that was an exaggeration. George still greets him sometimes.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Like when they made small talk during the Baku ceremony after Max came in late. Or during the Spanish GP when George congratulated him on his podium. Or the little nods, the stray looks, the eye contact they made that lasted a second too longâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The point was, it wasn't enough.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max can be greedy. He knows that. Especially when it comes to George. Give him a glance, and he wanted a smile. Give him a smile, and he wanted a talk. Give him a talk, and he wanted to touch.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So Max did something about it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He started saying George's name in every interview that would let him. "<em>George did quite well this weekend." "George is just a good driver in general."</em> He kept his tone light, casually tossing the name around. He did it often enough that a journalist finally took the bait and asked if they were close.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A strange calm settled in his chest as he leaned into the microphone. "I remember karting with him back then," he replied, keeping his face unreadable. "He wasâ€¦memorable."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>From then on, Max watched him. He looked at George's way from across the grid, across the briefing room, and across the pitlaneâ€” tallying each time George's eyes met his.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>At 2 AM, at his most desperate times, he'd tried messaging that old number of George's.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He typed one sentence, then deleted it. Typed another and deleted it again. He kept going on like that until he ended up sending something stupid, like a picture of his cats or whatever he was eating at the moment. There was never a response.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Strangely, he didn't feel humiliated by it. He wasn't asking for a reply, and George didn't owe him one. The messages only had to exist, a small proof that Max was still trying.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It all changed on one random afternoon.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The briefing finally ended, and the room broke apart into its usual shapes. Some drivers hovered around the corners, others left in twos and threes. Max was among those people who left afterwards. If he was honest, he only left cause he was following Albon back to the motorhome. Albon was walking beside George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Then they split off. Albon kept walking straight ahead while George took a different turn. Max hesitated on what he was going to do. Should he go back with Albon? Should he follow George?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Before he could make up his mind, his body already moved. Max pursed his lips and let out that same two-tone whistle he'd used for years to drag George out from a crowd.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George froze instantly. And so did a few nearby drivers who had grown up racing with them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Please. </em>Standing there, in the middle of the road, Max began praying to nobody and everybody. <em>God, Zeus, Odin, Brahma. Whoever it is up there. Make him look at me. Let him come to me again.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For a second, nothing happened.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Then, slowly, George moved. His shoulders came around first, then his head, and then those steel-blue eyes found Max's. He hesitated for a while. Slowly, almost shyly, his hand lifted to offer a small wave.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It felt like the world had finally righted itself.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>It works. </em>Max's pulse was ringing in his ears. It still works. <em>He still answers to me.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max raised his own hand in response. He had to keep himself from bouncing on his toes and breaking out into a wide grin.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max could put a lot of effort into things he was passionate about. Photography, for example. That same silver Kodak had survived years of being carried across half the world, and it still worked perfectly.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He never bothered hiding it. He stood openly at the balcony rail of the Red Bull motorhome, camera raised, aimed at the man walking downstairs.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George wore black sunglasses, a Williams polo shirt, and skinny denim jeans. He hadn't noticed Max yet.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max lifted the camera right when the sun hit his hair.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>People had always said that George was pretty, but Max hated the word.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was far too simple for him. It was something you would say to a flower or a girl on a magazine. Not that Max tried to find an alternative. He couldn't. Because it wasn't just one thing that made George "pretty".</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The hair first. It was a shade between brown and blonde depending on the light, and doused heavily with far too much gel. Max wished he would put it down. When they were kids, those curls had been his favorite thing to mess with. He'll probably never forgive whoever did George's hairstyle right now. Then, the mouth, full and pink and a little sweet at the edges. And those lashes were ridiculous. They made him look like Bambi.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Through the viewfinder, Max could see George tip his head up, a hand hovering to shade his face. He looked at Max and at the camera in his hands. Max waited for the frown. It didn't come. Instead, George posed, giving Max a small smile and a thumbs up.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max took a photo of that too.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>This is the one. This is what I'm keeping.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He wouldn't argue if anyone called him delusional. But this was permission too, wasn't it?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid12"><div class="polaroid12_front"></div><div class="polaroid12_back"><div class="polaroid12_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext2">Beautiful as always</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max looked at his closet. For the first time in a while, he reached past his plain black shirts and took out the Red Bull polo and a pair of jeans.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You've got to be kidding me."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There wasâ€¦there was an abomination on the plate right in front of him. <em>Jellied eels</em>, Lando said. It looked so slimy. And horrible. It was literally glistening under the pub lights, sitting in its little pool of gray-green jelly. <em>Is this really what the British eat?</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Yes, it is," George said cheerfully. He speared a piece with his fork and took a small bite like it was nothing. He then took a few more pieces and held them out to Max. "Now go on."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Nuh uh. Nope.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I don't love you enough to try that."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Hm." George's mouth twitched. He didn't lower the fork. "That's a bit disappointing to hear."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max tilted his head and gave him a look that he usually reserved for the cameras and sponsors. "You could always try and change my mind."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The pinkness that spread to George's ear was simply lovely.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Ew." Lando bit into his food with his chin propped on his palm and sporting the most unamused look Max had ever seen on him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Shut the hell up, Lando." Max didn't look away from George. "And you, put that thing away."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh, come on." The jelly wiggled to the movement of George's hand. George leaned forward, close enough that Max could smell him. "Just try it once. It's one of my favorites."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was doing everything he could to lean back without looking like he was leaning back. So far, he had lied, stalled, pointed vaguely at his stomach, and said something about diets. George only raised an eyebrow and kept the fork exactly where it was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>One of my favorites.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max closed his eyes and breathed in through his nose.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He's really doing this. He's really going to eat this slimy, boneless, cold fish-thingy because George wanted him to, because George invited him here, and because it was George's favorite meal.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He slightly opened his mouth, his hand gripping the wooden table when the food hit his mouth.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>You can't even call this food.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The thing was uncomfortably cold. It was soft in all the wrong ways and was quite literally slipping and sliding around in his mouth before he could even chew. To his surprise, the flavor itself wasn't half-bad. It was savory, salty, rich, and very fishy. But the texture was a fucking nightmare. There was something so jarring about biting from a firm, tender eel to the cold, slick jelly broth. Max tightened his jaw to fight back a violent gag.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He couldn't spit it out. He couldn't disrespect George like that. Not when he was watching him with that expectant, hopeful look.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So he swallowed hard.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"It'sâ€”" Max's voice came out slightly strangled. "It's good."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was silent for a while. Then Lando snorted into his drink, and George's face crumpled. The two of them fell apart laughing at the same time.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh, my god. Did you see his face?" Lando wheezed, thumping the table.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George pressed the back of his hand to his mouth. His shoulders were shaking, and the red color climbed up high on the peaks of his cheeks. "Sorry, Max. No one actually eats this."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max immediately spat out whatever remnants of the food were left in his mouth into a tissue. "Oh, fuck you two."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But he was helplessly smiling at George, who was still laughing at him with bright, creased eyes. It had been a long time since George looked at him like that.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max would have eaten the whole thing if it meant he could see it again.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Maybe just half.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="polaroid13"><div class="polaroid13_front"></div><div class="polaroid13_back"><div class="polaroid13_message"><p><br>
</p><p><span class="colortext3">LMFAOOO Got your ass</span></p><p><span class="colortext2">Watch yourself</span></p></div></div></div><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The room was too warm. The woman he was with was lying on her stomach beneath him. Her hair was stuck to the back of her neck as Max thrust into her in a hard, steady rhythm. He dragged his nose along the line of her spine and up into the hollow under her ear, trying to catch a faint whiff of that scent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Not her scent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>George's</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That bittersweet fragrance clung to her skin and made his teeth ache.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Frankly, he doesn't really know her name. He only knew her as the one who had been on George's arm for weeks. That was, until she crossed the crowded club room and came to Max, wrapped up in that scent like an invitation.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He should have turned her away. But then she'd stood close enough for it to reach him that he lost his goddamn mind.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She moaned, high and sweet, and breathy, and the sound scraped along his nerves.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Everything about this was wrong. She was too soft, too eager, and too easy. Her voice was so cloying when all he wanted was George. <em>He wanted George</em>. His type was a lower and rougher voice, one that would fight him for every sound.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He slid two fingers between her lips to fill her mouth and cut off the sound. She took them without complaint, and soon enough, her tongue began curling around them. She pushed her hips back in tandem with him, but it didn't help. None of her actions reached him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He pressed his face harder into the crook of her neck, chasing that scent down. But it was thinning with every breath, fading into her perfume and into the sheets. The more he took in, the less there was.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p> <em>Pathetic.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He heard it in his own voice.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He was an Alpha. A Verstappen. He'd never had to take anything secondhand in his life. He'd never had to scavenge or settle for scraps. And here he was, grinding into a stranger because she'd spent a few weeks with the one man he could never be with.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She gasped as she arched into his touch, her nails dragging at his arms, and he felt almost none of it. He was inside her, but the rest of him was somewhere else entirely.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max shut his eyes and inhaled again, deeper this time, and he hated himself a little more when it worked.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was in a 2.2-meter steel box, and he had been trapped in it for the better part of a minute. The air was already warm and suffocating. The maintenance voice on the intercom said <em>20 minutes</em>, though he personally knew how slow these people worked.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So unfortunately, he was stuck. Fortunately, he was stuck with George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George slid down the wall almost immediately and folded himself cross-legged on the floor. Then he said. "Okay. Word association. Go."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Are we twelve?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max complained out loud, but really, it was more for his dignity rather than an actual refusal.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Settlement."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max sighed through his nose. "Money. Praise."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Woof."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max was baffled for a few seconds before he said, "What the fuck?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George raised his hands up in mock surrender. "What? Praise. Good boy. Dog. Woof."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I really want to open your skull and look around. It's a goddamn mystery box in there."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"You're just mad I surprised you with that answer."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He <em>was</em> surprised. Max leaned back against the wall and crossed his arms, mostly to give his hands a place to be. In a space this small, their scents had nowhere else to go. The raw sting of petrol mixed with the crisp citrus and herbals, filling up his senses and working its way under his skin.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Good boy.</em> Max shook his head to push the thought away.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George shuffled from where he was sitting. "It's my turn, yeah?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Hit me with it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Foot."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Lick." Max paused, but the word was already out before he could take it back. "No, wait."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Too late. George was already dead on the floor, slapping it repeatedly with his palm as he wheezed. Max could feel the tips of his ears burning, but when George wiped his eyes and grinned at him, he couldn't find it in himself to be ashamed. He was having too much fun.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Had enough yet? Can I get my turn?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George waved him off, still laughing before giving him a thumbs up from the floor.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Blue."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Williams."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Suck up."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh, please," George grunted as he propped himself on his elbows. "I need that seat guaranteed."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max waved his arm at the empty space around them. "There's literally no one here but us."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George pointed towards the camera. "You never know, mate. Panic."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Disco."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Ah." And then, George immediately started butchering 'High Hopes' right in front of him. The song ended up sounding loud and flat. It really was a damn shame that he didn't have anything he could throw at him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Who lied to you and said you could sing?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George let his head fall back against the wall, sliding a few inches closer towards him. "I actually remember who did. His name starts with an M and ends with <em>ax Verstappen</em>."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He had this smile on his face like he was sharing a funny inside joke. Max felt his eyes drop before he could stop them. George's lips were pink, full, and a little chapped. Nothing Max couldn't fix. They kept inching closer, and closer, and Max didn't even breatheâ€”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A sudden noise from outside the elevator snapped them both out of their heads.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George immediately hit his elbow on the rail and swore. They scrambled to their feet, and by the time the doors opened, they were already standing at opposite corners of the box.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max walked past the maintenance crew, burning with irritation. He's petty enough to seriously consider tripping the nearest worker for their timing.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max established a rule for himself. It can't be anyone who George was currently seeing. It had to be someone he was done with. Never during.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The rule needed to be established because Max was an asshole and a fucking moron to boot.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Because it didn't stop at one, even though Max told himself it would. He said it the morning after the first. He said it again a month later, with a different guy. He said it the third time, then a fourth, and somewhere along the line, Max said it to himself the same way people would say <em>I'm fine</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>There was a pattern now. He could see it, and so could everyone else. That was probably why most of them would come to him after they broke it off with George. It was like they heard something about Max and wanted to see if it was true. Max never had to ask first.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A bottomless pit had opened in his stomach, and no amount of chardonnay could fill it up.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It was still there every time George would clap him on his back and ask how he's doing. It stayed there while Max smiled back and said something normal.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>You deserve nothing of him.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So he'd decide again that he'd stay away. He'd sit alone with the wanting and let himself starve.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Then someone new would drift across the room while wearing that scent, and Max would lie to himself again, thinking.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Just one more</em>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>George isn't stupid.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>His nose had always been sharp. Not as much as Max's, but sharp enough to set him apart.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The first time, you could still say it was a coincidence. He'd even joked about it with Max, still comfortable enough to tease him: "She likes roses, by the way."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But then it happened again, and again, and again.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Around the fourth time, the jokes stopped. George stopped bringing it up and stopped laughing. Max could feel how it was straining what was already a fragile relationship to begin with.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Across the paddock, their eyes met once. Those blue eyes were rimmed red on the edges, and dark bags dragged heavily under his eyes, showing his fatigue. He held the look for half a second. Then, George turned away.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ah.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He was back to square one.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>To no fault but his own.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria stood in the doorway with her arms crossed and watched as Max dug through all the boxes in the attic. She watched as he drove himself insane, trying to find every single thing that he had kept hidden for the past few years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The dusty air made him sneeze twice, but he still kept going. She let him, because it was one of the most entertaining things she had seen all month. That was until Max finally pulled out a battered box from behind an empty cabinet. Someone had written 'Max's Stuff' across the side in marker and in a handwriting that was unmistakably a twelve-year-old's.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She cleared her throat. "Is this the only reason you came home?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Of course not." Max blew the dust off the box and lifted it up with a grunt. "I have your gift downstairs. Happy twentieth birthday, <span class="translate-toggle"><span class="translate-default">ezel</span><span class="translate-alt">donkey</span></span>."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Awww, you shouldn't have." Victoria trailed him down the stairs. "Clearly, you're busy with other things."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I'm not. It's called multitasking. Ever tried it?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She smacked the back of his head. She then looked at the box with open suspicion. "What are you going to do with that?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max set it down on the floor of his old bedroom. Victoria plopped into his bed, sideways, with one hand propped under her head and the other on her hip. "Justâ€¦reminiscing," he said.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She wheezed like it was the funniest answer he could have given her. "<em>Lmao</em>. You fucked up, didn't you?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ignore her. She's being stupid.</em> Max began rummaging through the box, though Victoria just kept on talking.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"How long has it been since you guys reconnected? How the hell did you fuck up already?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I didn't fuck up." He pulled out a stack of papers and figurines so that he'd have something to do with his hands.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Sure you didn't." She rolled around the mattress, making a mess of the sheets. "So what did <em>he</em> do then?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Nothing. I told you, I'm reminiscing."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He stopped.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>At the bottom of the box was a photobooth strip. It had four frames on it with a plain white background, and a line from The Ridleys ''Be with you" printed at the bottom.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>I just wanna be with you every single dayâ€¦</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Ah.</em> It was coming back to him now. If his memory was right, then George probably had the matching halfâ€” the one with a black background bearing the next lyrics: â€¦<em>For the rest of our lives.</em> Max hadn't thought about it in years, and now he could see it vividly on the back of his mind.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A pillow hit him in the face. "Cut the crap." Max must have gone silent for too long because Victoria started leaning in to look at what he was holding. "Oh, ew. You two were so cheesy."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max finally snapped out of it and shoved her face away by the cheek. "Mind your own business."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She wasn't deterred. She still stubbornly peered between his fingers, squinting at the strip. "Seriously? The arm heart pose?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"There was a countdown!" Max hit her forehead with it. "You try and think of four duo poses in ten seconds."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Well, I wouldn't have to. I would have planned out my poses before I stepped into the booth instead of doing it on the fly like you dumbasses."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max rolled his eyes and said nothing, tucking the photobooth strip in his pocket. Seeing him quiet must have disturbed her, because when she spoke again, the teasing was gone from her voice. "Seriously. What's going on? It's barely been a minute since you set foot in the house and the first thing you do is tear apart the attic."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He hated it when she got like this. It had a way of stripping away his defenses and forcing the truth right out of him. But what he'd done was awful and, quite frankly, fucking embarassing, and he really couldn't handle her judgment on top of everything else.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Just say it. Come on." She pulled her knees up onto the bed. "I won't make fun of you."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That was a lie, but Max sighed anyway and let himself settle on the floor. He rested his back against the wall so he was facing her. Talking helped. By the time he got to the end, he felt like a weight was lifted off his shoulders.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria looked at him for a long moment. "You're an idiot. You know that, right?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I know."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"And a piece of shit, too. Neither George nor those people deserve to be dragged around by whatever feelings you have."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I <em>know</em>."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"So, what the hell are you doing here? The first thing you should've done is stop whatever this is and prostrate yourself in front of George."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max's frustration flared hot in his chest. "You think I haven't tried?! I would have gotten on my knees if he gave me the chance. But he won't. I fucking <em>can't</em>. And it's my fucking fault that he's avoiding me. I know that. That's why I'm starting from the beginning."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Pardon?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Justâ€¦I don't know. I'm desperate at this point."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Oh. Given what you've told me, trust me. I could tell." Victoria pulled out a chocolate bar from her pocket, unwrapped it, and chewed with the same delight as someone enjoying a show. "What exactly is your plan?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He told her.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria slapped her thigh and wheezed so hard that she hit her head against the backboard. Yes. It's atrocious. He knows. She didn't have to laugh like that. "You <em>cannot </em>be serious. You think you can <em>happy birthday</em> your way back into his life?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Fuck. Well, why not?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"First of all, you're not even trying." She pressed her fingers closed in a shut-up motion before he could retort. "Nuh uh. Zip it. You aren't reaching out to him Max. You're hiding behind that ancient phone number you <em>know</em> isn't connected anymore. It's a cheap way to feel like you tried without risking an actual answer."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max clamped his mouth shut, bitterly fidgeting his thumb as the silence stretched between them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Well, do you have any suggestions?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Hell yeah, actually." She finished chewing and balled up the wrapper in her fist. "You've got to do it the old-fashioned way."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Max frowned the moment she said it. "How is that any different?"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"A letter's personal. You can tell a lot about someone from how they write, and it shows effort." She flicked the wrapper at his chest. "Handwritten <em>and</em> personalized. It's a million times better than a text."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"That'sâ€”"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"What? Embarassing? Humiliating? I thought you would do anything."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>He stared at the floor. "He won't accept it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"He probably won't." She said it flatly. "You might get rejected a few more times at the start. But being forgiven isn't supposed to be easy. Consider it as the consequences of your stupidity."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>Can't argue with that.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"And listen to me when I tell you this." She sat up and pointed at him. "If you're actually serious about this, you are going to try, and you are going to accept whatever answer he gives you. All of it. If he says he can't forgive you, then he can't, and you don't get to push him."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The only sound left in the room was the steady <em>whizz</em> of the fan. <em>Accept whatever answer he gives.</em> Max turned the words over in his head and immediately wanted to reject it. The truth was, he wasn't sure if he could handle hearing a "no" from George.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"Fine," he said at last.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Victoria arched a brow at him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>"I said fine. I'll accept it."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>She studied him for a few seconds, then flopped back down on the bed and unwrapped a new candy. "Good. Now do us both a favor and buy a good quality paper. And don't you dare write it tonight without me. You'll sound unhinged."</p><p>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><div class="letter"><div class="letter_cover"><div class="letter_cover_text"><p>&nbsp;</p><p align="center"><b>George Russell</b></p></div></div><div class="letter_top"><div class="letter_top_text"><p><br>
</p><p>George,<br>
I hope this letter finds you well. The other six letters Iâ€™ve written were worse.<br>
Iâ€™m sorry. I know thatâ€™s not enough, but I at least wanted to start with that. What I did to you, and to those girls, was cruel. I donâ€™t have a good reason for it, and I wonâ€™t insult you by inventing one.</p></div></div><div class="letter_mid"><div class="letter_mid_text"><p><br>
</p><p>I know itâ€™s shameless to ask you to forgive me, but Iâ€™m asking anyway. You were the first person who ever stood beside me, and I never thanked you for it. I think Iâ€™d spent most of my time assuming youâ€™d just be there, and I didnâ€™t know what to do with myself when you werenâ€™t. Thatâ€™s not an excuse either.</p></div></div><div class="letter_bottom"><div class="letter_bottom_text"><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Iâ€™m not asking for everything to suddenly go back to normal. I just hope that we could talk again, if youâ€™ll have it. Whenever you want, however you want. Iâ€™ll be at the next race either way. Iâ€™ll wait for you just in case you decide to come.<br>
I swear Iâ€™ll make it up to you for as long as you let me.
</p><p align="right"> Yours truly,<br>
Max</p></div></div></div><div class="player"><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arcielle224-png/redred/d5494bdc2ed927ea681834e42bfcc72770643734/be-with-you.jpg"></p><div class="info"><p class="title">Be With You</p><p class="artist">The Ridleys</p><audio controls="controls" crossorigin="anonymous" preload="metadata">
